kallsyms: hide the ksu/susfs symbols that SUSFS's prefix filter misses

SUSFS's CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS_HIDE_KSU_SUSFS_SYMBOLS filter in kernel/kallsyms.c
uses susfs_starts_with(), so it only catches names that BEGIN with one of its
prefixes. On this tree that leaves 18 KSU/SUSFS symbols visible in
/proc/kallsyms, verified on-device:

  # embedded, not prefixed -> missed by susfs_starts_with()
  __func__.susfs_init, __func__.susfs_show_version, __func__.susfs_show_variant,
  __func__.susfs_is_inode_sus_path, __func__.susfs_start_sdcard_monitor_fn,
  __func__.susfs_open_redirect_spoof_{do_proc_readlink,show_map_vma_srcu,vfs_statfs},
  __func__.ksu_handle_umount, anon_ksu_{fops,ioctl,release},
  check_nnp_nosuid.ksu_sid, do_ksu_load_allow_list, is_ksud_exists,
  is_task_ksu_domain, setup_ksu_cred, setup_ksu_cred_selinux

The 9 __func__.susfs_* entries are emitted by SUSFS's own SUSFS_LOGI/LOGE
macros (fs/susfs.c), i.e. SUSFS fails to hide itself; the rest come from the
ReSukiSU/KernelSU driver.

This adds substring matching in front of the existing prefix list:

  strstr(name, "_ksu") || strstr(name, ".ksu") || strstr(name, "susfs")

It deliberately does NOT match a bare "ksu": that substring also appears inside
"checksum"/"chksum", and hiding ~60 unrelated symbols (skb_checksum,
nf_ip_checksum, f2fs_inode_chksum, exfat_calc_chksum16, ...) would itself be a
detectable anomaly. The "_ksu"/".ksu" anchors match all 18 leaked names and
zero checksum symbols (both counts verified against the running kernel's
/proc/kallsyms).

The existing susfs_starts_with() entries are kept: names like "ksud",
"kernelsu", "getenforce", "setenforce", "try_umount", "is_zygote" contain
neither "ksu" nor "susfs" and still need prefix matching.

<linux/string.h> is included explicitly for strstr(); the file did not pull it
in directly before.

NOTE: not applied to the tree. Apply with:
  patch -p1 < patches/0001-susfs-hide-remaining-ksu-susfs-symbols-from-kallsyms.patch

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.8 <noreply@anthropic.com>
--- a/kernel/kallsyms.c	2026-09-06 16:49:48.635486625 +0000
+++ b/kernel/kallsyms.c	2026-09-06 16:49:48.668486736 +0000
@@ -24,6 +24,7 @@
 #include <linux/filter.h>
 #include <linux/compiler.h>
 #ifdef CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS_HIDE_KSU_SUSFS_SYMBOLS
+#include <linux/string.h>
 #include <linux/susfs_def.h>
 #endif // #ifdef CONFIG_KSU_SUSFS_HIDE_KSU_SUSFS_SYMBOLS
 
@@ -621,7 +622,20 @@
 			   iter->type, iter->name);
 #else
 	{
-		if (susfs_starts_with(iter->name, "ksu_") ||
+		/*
+		 * Prefix matching alone misses symbols that merely embed the
+		 * ksu/susfs names, e.g. compiler-generated "__func__.susfs_*"
+		 * and "anon_ksu_*", "do_ksu_*", "is_task_ksu_*", "is_ksud_*".
+		 *
+		 * Match "_ksu" and ".ksu" rather than a bare "ksu": the latter
+		 * also occurs inside "checksum"/"chksum", which would hide ~60
+		 * unrelated symbols (skb_checksum, nf_ip_checksum, f2fs_inode_chksum,
+		 * exfat_calc_chksum16, ...). Their absence is itself a signal.
+		 */
+		if (strstr(iter->name, "_ksu") ||
+			strstr(iter->name, ".ksu") ||
+			strstr(iter->name, "susfs") ||
+			susfs_starts_with(iter->name, "ksu_") ||
 			susfs_starts_with(iter->name, "__ksu_") ||
 			susfs_starts_with(iter->name, "susfs_") ||
 			susfs_starts_with(iter->name, "ksud") ||
